DESCRIPTION:Over the past couple of weeks\, you have built the full toolkit
  needed to construct a persuasive academic essay: deconstructing a prompt\
 , formulating a thesis\, outlining your ideas\, developing paragraphs with
  PEEL\, reasoning from credible evidence\, and avoiding logical fallacies.
  This assignment asks you to bring all of it together in a single\, polish
 ed argumentative essay on a topic grounded in the African context. This i
 s not simply a writing exercise. It is a test of whether you can take a po
 sition on a real\, contested issue affecting the continent\, defend it wit
 h credible evidence\, and engage seriously with the strongest opposing vie
 w\, rather than the easiest one to knock down.\n\n-------------------\nLea
 rning Objectives\n-------------------\n\nThis assignment assesses your abi
 lity to:\n\n* Construct a clear\, specific\, and arguable thesis statement
 .\n\n* Organise an essay using a logical introduction-body-conclusion stru
 cture.\n\n* Develop body paragraphs using the PEEL method\, with each para
 graph clearly supporting the thesis.\n\n* Integrate credible evidence and 
 explain its relevance through sound reasoning.\n\n* Acknowledge and respon
 d to a counterargument.\n\n* Apply correct APA in-text citation and refere
 ncing.\n\n* Write with clarity\, grammatical accuracy\, and an appropriate
  academic tone.\n\n-----------------\nChoose One Prompt\n-----------------
 \n\nSelect one of the following prompts. Each is deliberately broad enough
  to allow you to narrow it toward a specific angle or country/regional cas
 e\, which you should do in your thesis statement. Must include the title "
 A Different World"\n\n* Regional integration vs. bilateral trade: Do you t
 hink African governments should prioritise regional economic integration (
 e.g.\, through the African Continental Free Trade Area) over bilateral tra
 de agreements with non-African partners?\n\n* Platform regulation and demo
 cracy: Should social media platforms be more strictly regulated in African
  “democracies” to curb misinformation and protect the integrity of ele
 ctions?\n\n* Youth entrepreneurship vs. structural reform: Is promoting yo
 uth entrepreneurship the most effective solution to unemployment in Sub-Sa
 haran Africa\, or does it place too much responsibility on individuals rat
 her than systemic reform?\n\n* Aid conditionality and sovereignty: Can Afr
 ican governments push back more firmly against conditionalities attached t
 o foreign aid in order to protect national policy sovereignty?\n\n--------
 ----\nRequirements\n------------\n\n* Length: 1\,500 to 2\,000 words (excl
 uding reference list).\n\n* Structure: Introduction (hook\, background\, t
 hesis) → body paragraphs (PEEL-structured\, one per major supporting poi
 nt) → at least one counterargument-and-rebuttal paragraph → conclusion
  (restated thesis\, synthesis\, closing thought).\n\n* Evidence: A minimum
  of 6 credible sources (academic journals\, reputable news outlets\, insti
 tutional reports\, or recognised policy bodies)\, including at least 2 pee
 r-reviewed academic sources. Wikipedia\, personal blogs\, and AI chat outp
 uts are not acceptable sources.\n\n* Citation style: APA 7th Edition\, bot
 h in-text and in a full reference list.\n\n* Formatting: 12 pt Times New 
 Roman\, double-spaced\, page numbers.\n\n \n\n------------------\nAcadem
 ic Integrity\n------------------\n\nThis assignment must reflect your own 
 original thinking\, research\, and writing. As per course policy\, submiss
 ions with an AI-detection score above 20% will be capped at half marks. Du
 plicate submissions or unattributed use of others' work will be treated as
  plagiarism under ALU's academic integrity policy. If you use AI tools to 
 brainstorm or check grammar\, this must be declared per module guidelines.
 \n\n----------\nSubmission\n----------\n\nSubmit as a single PDF or Word d
 ocument through the course portal by the stated deadline. Late submissions
  are subject to the standard late-penalty policy. 
